the first anniversary of Sudan's  military takeover and thousands gathered  to demonstrate in the capital Khartoum  and other cities protesters demanding  the Army and its Rule and hand over  power to a civilian government  [Music]  we want to return to a democratic  civilian transition the Army should  return to the barracks and militia  groups should be dissolved we don't want  them to rule they should know they are  supposed to only preserve the country's  security not take over power  the coup ended a two-year transitional  government that was a result of a power  sharing agreement between the Army and  the pro-democracy group forces of  freedom and change or FFC the military  then deposed prime minister Abdullah  hamdulk and arrested several senior  government officials they were later  released and hamdurim stated before he  resigned in January the Takeover angered  many on the streets  we are out today to support the demand  of a civilian government that came as a  result of the Revolution it was leading  the country to a democracy we're not  against the Army as a whole just some  individuals the Army has been  politicized and if there are reforms  made to it we will not have any issues  because there is no country without an  army  the Takeover also created a political  turmoil as some parties sided with the  military in its power grab and others  condemned it with the African Union the  United Nations Mission here in Sudan and  the regional body intergovernmental  Authority for development have been  trying to broker a deal between the  political parties and the military  but that has so far failed to produce  results Security Forces use tear gas to  disperse protesters since the Takeover  more than 100 people have been killed  and hundreds injured in protests against  the military but that's not stop the  demonstrators  the Takeover has not produced a single  good thing the health and education  sector are deteriorating and there are  more tribal conflicts the social fabric  of Sudan has been destroyed  Sudan's Army has previously stated it  was stepping away from talks to allow  parties to reach a consensus on forming  a new government while that is yet to  happen those on the streets continue to  call for a return to civilian Rule  people Morgan Al Jazeera
 

